.TH CWLOGS-RETENTION 1 "2026-11-19" "cwlogs-retention 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cwlogs-retention \- price CloudWatch Logs storage from describe-log-groups JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
Estimate CloudWatch Logs storage cost from describe-log-groups JSON. Reads files only, no network.

Usage: cwlogs-retention [OPTIONS] <FILE>...

Arguments:
  <FILE>...  `describe-log-groups` JSON files, gzip exports, directories, or `-` for standard input

Options:
      --prices <FILE>              Price file. The default is the built-in CloudWatch Logs storage list
      --as-of <YYYY-MM-DD>         Date ages are measured to, `YYYY-MM-DD`, midnight UTC. Without it, savings are omitted
      --fail-stored-bytes <BYTES>  Exit 1 when a group with no retention has storedBytes greater than this
      --json                       Print JSON instead of the text table
      --md                         Print a Markdown table instead of the text table
      --output <FILE>              Write the report to this file instead of standard output. An existing file is replaced
  -q, --quiet                      Print only the result and errors: no notes, no warnings
  -v, --verbose                    Add progress lines on standard error
  -h, --help                       Print help
  -V, --version                    Print version

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 unretained group above --fail-stored-bytes, 2 input or usage error, 3 --quiet with --verbose or --json with --md.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
cwlogs-retention reads local
.B aws logs describe-log-groups
output and prices the archived storage of each log group from its
.B storedBytes
and its log group class.
It lists the groups that never expire and estimates what keeping 30, 90, or 365 days would save per month if ingest stays steady.
That estimate is not a forecast.
.PP
The tool reads files only.
It makes no network call, uses no AWS SDK, and does not follow
.BR nextToken .
The advice lines show an
.B aws logs put-retention-policy
command and say it was not executed.
The tool never runs it.
.PP
The input is a JSON file, a gzip file, a directory of such files, or
.B -
for standard input.
A file is read as gzip when its first bytes are the gzip magic 1f 8b, whatever its name.
Bytes after the first gzip member are an error.
A file larger than 268435456 bytes is rejected.
An explicit symlink is followed.
A symlink inside a directory is skipped with a warning, and a directory nested deeper than 64 levels is an error.
The account id in an ARN is not printed.
.PP
A log group needs a
.B logGroupName
of 1 to 512 characters from the CloudWatch name alphabet and a
.B storedBytes
that is a plain run of digits.
.B retentionInDays
is a whole number from 1 to 3653, as a number or as a string of digits; a value that CloudWatch does not offer, such as 8, is shown with a warning.
Arrays and objects nested 128 or more levels deep are not JSON.
A number given as a string is at most 20 characters, also with leading zeros.
The total of the stored bytes of all groups must fit in 18446744073709551615; a larger total is an error, \fBstored bytes overflow\fR.
A price file value is a plain decimal of at most 40 characters, with at most 28 digits after the point and at most 28 significant digits.
.PP
In a directory, a member that is a text file, is not UTF-8, or is empty is skipped with a warning.
A member that is valid JSON without log groups is an error.
With
.BR --fail-stored-bytes ,
any skipped member is an error, because the total would be missing it.
.PP
The built-in storage rates are the us-east-1 examples of the CloudWatch pricing page, read on 2026-11-09.
Amounts are computed exactly and rounded once to the cent, half away from zero; an amount that does not fit is a price overflow and not a cut amount (see the README, Money precision).
The 5 GB account free tier is not subtracted.
When the date of the price data is more than 180 days before --as-of, or before today without --as-of, a warning goes to standard error and the exit code stays 0.
The environment variable CWLOGS_RETENTION_TODAY=YYYY-MM-DD sets the day used as today.
The monthly cost is an estimate from list prices and not your AWS invoice: it is named estimated_usd_month, the text output says so in one line, and the JSON has "estimate": true.
Ingestion, Logs Insights, Live Tail, data-protection scanning, and vended delivery tiers are not priced.
.PP
The whole file is held in memory with all of its fields, also those the tool does not use: about 53 times the file size for a list of numbers in an unknown field.
Keep exports to the groups you need.
In a path or an argument that is not UTF-8, each invalid byte is shown as
.BR \exNN ;
control and direction characters are shown as
.BR \eu{...} .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --prices \fIFILE\fR
A price file with a source, a date, a region, and a rate per log group class.
The default is the built-in list.
.TP
.B --as-of \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
The date, midnight UTC, to which log group ages are measured.
Without it, or without
.BR creationTime ,
the storage cost is shown and the savings are omitted.
.TP
.B --fail-stored-bytes \fIBYTES\fR
Exit 1 when a group with no retention has
.B storedBytes
greater than
.IR BYTES .
A group that has retention does not trip it.
The report is still printed.
.TP
.B --json
Print one JSON document instead of the text table.
Money is written as strings.
The first key is
.BR schema_version ,
the whole number 1.
Warnings go to standard error.
.TP
.B --output \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to
.I FILE
instead of standard output, which stays empty.
The report goes to a new temporary file in the same directory and is renamed over
.IR FILE ,
so
.I FILE
is either the old report or the whole new one.
An existing file is replaced; a symbolic link at
.I FILE
is replaced and its target is not followed or changed.
A missing directory is not created, and a directory at
.I FILE
is refused.
A failed write is exit 2 with a message on standard error, and the temporary file is removed.
Exit 1 from
.B --fail-stored-bytes
still writes the file.
.TP
.B --md
Print a Markdown table instead of the text table, with the same amounts, one estimate line, and the notes.
A pipe, a backslash, or a line break in a cell is escaped.
With
.B --quiet
only the estimate line and the table are printed.
.B --json
with
.B --md
is a usage error with exit 3.
.TP
.B -q, --quiet
Print only the result and errors.
The text output is the estimate line, the table, and the threshold lines when
.B --fail-stored-bytes
is set; notes, advice, and warnings are left out, and no warning goes to standard error.
The JSON document is not shortened.
The exit codes do not change.
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
Add progress lines on standard error, each starting with
.BR progress: .
Standard output does not change.
.B --quiet
with
.B --verbose
is a usage error with exit 3.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the usage text.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was written.
.TP
.B 1
A group with no retention has more stored bytes than the
.B --fail-stored-bytes
threshold.
The report is still printed.
.TP
.B 2
An input or usage error: an unreadable file, bad JSON, an invalid field, a duplicate log group name, a skipped directory member while a threshold is set, a bad option, or a standard output that is closed or cannot be written.
The message goes to standard error and does not include file contents.
A closed standard output is detected on Linux only; standard output on
.I /dev/null
still exits 0.
With a closed standard output, a usage error or an input error is still shown on standard error.
.TP
.B 3
--quiet and --verbose, or --json and --md, were both given.
Nothing is read and standard output stays empty.
.TP
.B 130
On Unix, SIGINT (Ctrl-C) ended the run.
Standard output is empty and standard error has the line
.BR "cwlogs-retention: interrupted by SIGINT" .
A report that is already being written is finished first.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cwlogs-retention examples/describe-log-groups.json
cwlogs-retention --as-of 2026-11-09 --json logs/
aws logs describe-log-groups | cwlogs-retention --fail-stored-bytes 107374182400 -
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
README.md and QUALITY.md in the source tree.
